          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em directed topological space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in which there is some ‘sense of direction’. This can happen in various different ways, and the level of the ‘directedness’ can be different in different situations, so there are several ‘competing’ ideas, but the beginning of a consensus on what the overarching idea is.
          %p
            If one bases homotopy theory on the idea of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplex"} singular simplex
            or, more generally, a singular cell of any shape, then there is no way in which a ‘sense of direction’ can be encoded. If we have a path in a space, we can travel along it in either direction, from 0 to 1 or from 1 to 0. From this perspective, a directed space is one in which not every singular cell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n \to X
            (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
            the standard topological
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            %em traversable
            in all directions, in some sense: instead, these
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %em paths
            may have a
            = succeed "." do
              %em direction
          %p
            As an example, one can base the ‘sense of direction’ on a closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            (that is, a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pospace"} pospace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            with the usual order to the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            can only be ‘traversed’ in one direction. Another example which does not fit into this first type would be the directed circle.
