              %path#path3164{:d => "M 479.08786,236.21529 C 479.08786,259.73666 459.99806,278.82647 436.47668,278.82647 C 412.95531,278.82647 393.86551,259.73666 393.86551,236.21529 C 393.86551,212.69392 412.95531,193.60411 436.47668,193.60411 C 459.99806,193.60411 479.08786,212.69392 479.08786,236.21529 z", :style => "fill:none;fill-opacity:1;fill-rule:evenodd;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:0.99921262;stroke-linecap:butt;stroke-linejoin:miter;marker:none;marker-start:url(#Arrow1Lstart);marker-mid:none;marker-end:none;stroke-miterlimit:4;stroke-dasharray:none;stroke-dashoffset:0;stroke-opacity:1;visibility:visible;display:inline;overflow:visible;enable-background:accumulate"}
          %p In other words, a circle with direction determined by the anticlockwise sense. Again, it is easy to see that there are certain paths that respect the direction while others do not.
          %p
            So far, there exists a well-developed theory for a notion of
            %em directed spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            where 1-dimensional paths given by maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \to X
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            into the space are equipped with a direction. See in particular the book by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"} Marco Grandis
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Directed+Algebraic+Topology"} Directed Algebraic Topology
            listed below. Another suggested notion for modelling directed spaces is that of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#framed_spaces"} framed spaces
          %p
            Note that a directed space is like a generalised space; not every directed space need be a space in the traditional sense, in accordance with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"} Marco Grandis
            in his book
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Directed+Algebraic+Topology"} Directed Algebraic Topology
            handles the directed homotopy of small categories, and of cubical complexes, since this is useful for comparison an interpretation of directed homotopy ‘invariants’.
          %p
            Directed spaces are studied in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+space"} directed space
            has a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+category"} fundamental category
          %h3#homotopytheoretic_perspective Homotopy-theoretic perspective
          %p
            From a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            perspective, one would wish that notions of directed spaces might serve to generalize the
            = succeed "\u2014which" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "\u2014to" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C0%29-category"} (∞,0)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C0%29-category"} (∞,0)-categories
            are generalized to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,r)-categories
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \gt 0
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cr%29-category"} (∞,r)-category
            in this context might correspond to an
            = succeed "\u2014one" do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
                \-directed topological space
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
