          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %ul
            %li
              If we can equip directed spaces with an internal hom, then a directed space with at least one directed path should be a strictly directed object in the category of directed spaces, with respect to the standard directed interval as the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undirected+object"} undirected object
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+object"} directed object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+type+theory"} directed homotopy type theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The above definition of
            = succeed "-spaces" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"}> Marco Grandis
              ,
              %em Directed homotopy theory, I. The fundamental category
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AT/0111048"}> arXiv
              )
          %p This has now developed into a book
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"}> Marco Grandis
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Directed+Algebraic+Topology"}> Directed Algebraic Topology
                , Models of non-reversible worlds
              , Cambridge University Press, 2009.
          %p A discussion of reparameterization of directed paths in directed topological spaces is in
          %ul
            %li
              Ulrich Fahrenberg and Martin Raussen,
              %em Reparametrizations of Continuous Paths
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.3560"}> arXiv
              , (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/09/fahrenberg_and_raussen_on_cont.html"}> blog
              ))
          %p
            Further references are given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li
              Emmanuel Haucourt,
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2012.05.008"}
                %em> Streams, d-Spaces and Their Fundamental Categories
                , Electronic Notes in Theoretical Computer Science, Volume 283, 15 June 2012, Pages 111-151
          %p Some ‘local combinatorial’ aspects of framed spaces are discussed in:
          %ul
            %li#DornDouglas21
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Dorn"} Christoph Dorn
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christopher+Douglas"} Christopher Douglas
              = succeed "," do
                %em Framed combinatorial topology
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.14700"} arXiv
          %p A global (higher-categorical) perspective on directed spaces is taken in:
          %ul
            %li#Dorn23
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Dorn"}> Christoph Dorn
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Nine short stories about geometric higher categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://cxdorn.github.io/assets/pdfs/nine-stories.pdf"} pdf
